    "title": "Chapter 2: Kinematics in One Dimension",
    "definitionTitle": "Definition: Position and Reference Frame",
    "definition": "Position is the location of an object relative to a chosen origin, together with its direction. In one dimension it is written as a signed coordinate \\(x\\). A reference frame (or frame of reference) is the coordinate system, including its origin and positive direction, from which positions and velocities are measured. A measurement of position or velocity has meaning only after the reference frame has been stated.",
    "conceptTitle": "Key Concept: Distance versus Displacement",
    "concept": "Feature Distance Displacement Type Scalar Vector Depends on The entire path taken Only the initial and final positions Sign Always positive or zero Positive, negative, or zero Magnitude Never less than \\(|\\Delta x|\\) Never greater than the distance The magnitude of the displacement equals the distance only when the motion is along a straight line without any reversal of direction.",
    "exampleTitle": "Example 2.1: Total Distance Walked",
    "example": "A person walks \\(5\\ \\text{m}\\) to the east, turns around, and walks \\(3\\ \\text{m}\\) to the west. The total distance travelled is the sum of the lengths of both parts of the path: \\(\\text{Distance} = 5\\ \\text{m} + 3\\ \\text{m} = 8\\ \\text{m}\\) Distance records how much ground was covered, not where the person ended up.",
